# a defaulted parameter whose type is a type variable: the default literal does not adapt to the variable once a call
# binds it - "alpha <T> = 1" is error[E12] "expected <T>, found I32" at the declaration, though every call binding T
# to a number could take 1 as a literal of T (G9a adapts a literal argument the same way). std/linalg's Gemm takes
# alpha and beta as F64 for this reason, converted to the element type once per call.
fn scaled(x Array<<T>>&, alpha <T> = 1) <T> { return x[0] * alpha }

fn main() ? {
    a := F32[2.0, 3.0]
    assert scaled(a) == 2.0
    assert scaled(a, 0.5) == 1.0
}
